    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">1099 Loan vs Bank Statement Loan for Freelancers: How to Choose the Right One for Your Income Type</h1>

    <p class="mt-8">If you've figured out that conventional financing isn't the right path for your income profile and you're looking at Non-QM options, you've likely encountered two products that both seem designed for someone like you: a 1099 loan and a bank statement loan. They're not the same product, they don't suit the same income type, and choosing between them requires understanding how each one handles the specific way your freelance income arrives and gets documented.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Core Difference Between These Two Products</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A 1099 loan qualifies you based on the gross income shown on your 1099 forms from clients. It uses the amount clients reported paying you before any deductions, which is typically higher than what your Schedule C shows as net income. A bank statement loan qualifies you based on your actual deposits over 12 to 24 months, applying an expense ratio to arrive at qualifying income. Both approaches can produce a higher qualifying number than conventional underwriting. Which one produces the higher number for you depends on how your income arrives and how it's documented.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">Why Not All Freelance Income Arrives on a 1099</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Not all freelance income arrives on a 1099. A writer who receives royalty payments from multiple publishers, a photographer who sells prints through an online platform, a designer who gets paid through a marketplace, a musician who receives streaming royalties through a distributor: much of that income may not generate a traditional client-issued 1099, either because individual payments fall below the reporting threshold or because the income comes through a platform that aggregates payments before issuing a single annual form.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">For freelancers whose income is documented primarily through 1099s from identifiable clients, a 1099 loan is often the cleaner path. For freelancers whose income is a mix of 1099s, platform payouts, direct transfers, and smaller payments that never generated a formal 1099, a bank statement loan captures the full picture more accurately.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">When a 1099 Loan Is the Stronger Choice</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A 1099 loan works best when your gross 1099 income is significantly higher than your net Schedule C income after deductions, your 1099 income comes from a consistent set of clients over 12 to 24 months, your 1099 forms document most or all of your freelance earnings, and your actual business expenses are high enough that a bank statement expense ratio would reduce your qualifying income below the 1099 gross figure.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A copywriter who works with three regular agency clients, each of whom issues a 1099 annually, and who has significant deductible expenses, may find that the gross 1099 figure produces a meaningfully higher qualifying income than a bank statement calculation after the expense ratio is applied.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">When a Bank Statement Loan Is the Stronger Choice</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">A bank statement loan works best when your income comes from multiple sources that don't all generate 1099s, your actual overhead is low enough that an expense ratio below 50% could apply, your deposit history is clean and consolidated in a single account, and your gross deposits over 12 to 24 months represent a strong qualifying income even after the expense ratio.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">A freelance illustrator who earns through a combination of direct client work, print sales through an online shop, and licensing fees from stock platforms may not have 1099s that capture the full picture. A single business account receiving all of those payments will show a deposit history that does.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Calculate your gross 1099 income for the last 12 and 24 months.</strong> That's your 1099 loan qualifying income estimate. Compare it to your Schedule C net income to see how much higher the 1099 approach gets you.</li>
      <li><strong>Calculate your total deposits for the last 12 and 24 months and apply a 50% expense ratio.</strong> That's your bank statement qualifying income estimate. Compare both numbers and identify which produces the stronger qualification.</li>
      <li><strong>Assess how completely your 1099 forms capture your total income.</strong> If there are meaningful income streams that don't generate 1099s, a bank statement loan captures those where a 1099 loan does not.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluate your actual expense ratio.</strong> If your real business overhead is well below 50% of revenue, document it. A lower expense ratio applied to your deposits may produce a bank statement qualifying income that exceeds your 1099 gross.</li>
      <li><strong>Shop both products with at least one Non-QM lender before deciding.</strong> Some lenders offer both, some specialize in one. Understanding the rate and eligibility differences between the two products at a specific lender gives you a concrete basis for comparison rather than a theoretical one.</li>
    </ol>

